# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Container image for apps/api (the Fastify server).
#
# ## The base image is pinned by digest
#
# `node:24.18.0-alpine3.24` is a tag, and a tag is repointed every time the
# upstream image is rebuilt for a base-OS patch. That is usually a good thing and
# occasionally the delivery mechanism for something else, and either way it means
# two builds of this file at different times are not the same build. The digest
# below is the multi-arch OCI index for that tag (linux/amd64, linux/arm64/v8,
# linux/s390x), captured 2026-08-25.
#
# To refresh it (no Docker daemon required, and it does not pull the image):
#
#     TOKEN=$(curl -s 'https://auth.docker.io/token?service=registry.docker.io&scope=repository:library/node:pull' \
#       | node -e "let d='';process.stdin.on('data',c=>d+=c).on('end',()=>process.stdout.write(JSON.parse(d).token))")
#     curl -sI -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
#       -H 'Accept: application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json' \
#       -H 'Accept: application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json' \
#       https://registry-1.docker.io/v2/library/node/manifests/24.18.0-alpine3.24 \
#       | grep -i docker-content-digest
#
# With the daemon: `docker buildx imagetools inspect node:24.18.0-alpine3.24`.
# Keep the human-readable tag in the reference as well as the digest, so a reader
# can see which version they are looking at without resolving the hash.
#
# ## Why Alpine, when this file used to say bookworm-slim
#
# The base was `node:24.18.0-bookworm-slim@sha256:6f7b03f7…51452d` until
# 2026-08-25. It was replaced because the runtime stage descends from `base`, so
# the OS package set of the SHIPPED images is the base's — and that package set
# was the entire vulnerability surface of this project's release artefacts.
#
# Measured with trivy 0.72.0, `--severity HIGH,CRITICAL --scanners vuln`, against
# the BASE IMAGES alone on arm64, before any application layer:
#
#   base                              OS pkgs  H/C findings  unfixed  fixable
#   node:24.18.0-bookworm-slim (old)      88     30 (26/4)       30        0
#   node:24.18.0-trixie-slim              79     51 (48/3)       15       36
#   node:24.18.0-alpine3.24 (this)        18      0 (0/0)         0        0
#
# Across the two shipped images the old base contributed 60 HIGH/CRITICAL
# findings — 52 HIGH, 8 CRITICAL, 14 distinct CVEs — every one of them from the
# base and none from an application layer. All 60 are gone, and none of the 14
# CVEs exists in the Alpine package set.
#
# Trixie was considered and rejected, and the reason is the opposite of the
# obvious one: 36 of its findings HAVE upstream fixes, and this file runs no
# `apt-get` at any stage, so under this repository's gate policy (block when a fix
# exists — see scripts/supply-chain/container-scan.mjs) trixie would have turned a
# passing gate into 72 blocking findings. Distroless was rejected because both
# runtime stages need a shell for their `RUN rm -rf`, it puts node at
# `/nodejs/bin/node` (so `docker run --entrypoint node` breaks, and so does
# `checkArtefact` in scripts/release-artifacts.mjs), and it has no `node` user —
# which breaks `USER node`, the CI assertion on `Config.User`, and the
# `uid=1000,gid=1000` tmpfs line in docker-compose.staging.yml.
#
# ## Why the switch was cheap here — the things that had to be true
#
#   * Nothing compiles native code. pnpm-workspace.yaml sets `ignoreScripts: true`
#     and `onlyBuiltDependencies: []`, and every install below also passes
#     `--ignore-scripts`. node-gyp never runs, so there is no glibc-linked
#     addon to rebuild.
#   * pnpm-lock.yaml already carries every musl variant it needs
#     (`@next/swc-linux-{x64,arm64}-musl`, `@img/sharp-linuxmusl-{x64,arm64}`,
#     `@biomejs/cli-linux-*-musl`) and there is no `supportedArchitectures` block
#     pinning a libc, so pnpm resolves by install platform and
#     `--frozen-lockfile` is still satisfied.
#   * `node:24-alpine` has `uid=1000(node) gid=1000(node)`, identical to the
#     Debian images, so `USER node` still produces `Config.User == "node"`.
#   * node is at `/usr/local/bin/node` on both, and neither Dockerfile referenced
#     a Debian path, `dpkg`, or `apt-get`.
#
# ## What Alpine changes that a reader should know
#
#   * There is no bash. `/bin/sh` is busybox ash, which is what Docker's default
#     `SHELL ["/bin/sh", "-c"]` was already using — every `RUN` in this file is
#     POSIX and stays POSIX. If you add one, no `[[ ]]`, no arrays, no `pipefail`.
#     busybox `find` does support `-mindepth`/`-maxdepth`/`-exec … +`, which the
#     prod-deps prune below relies on; that was checked, not assumed.
#   * `wget` exists here (busybox provides it) where bookworm-slim had neither
#     wget nor curl. The healthcheck at the bottom is still `node -e` anyway —
#     see the reason written there, which was never really about availability.
#   * `apk` itself is left in place. It is a package manager in a production
#     image, which this file otherwise objects to, but deleting it would also
#     remove the apk database trivy reads to identify the OS, and
#     container-scan.mjs correctly FAILS a scan whose OS it cannot identify —
#     trading a real gate for a cosmetic one. The runtime root filesystem is
#     read-only and the process is uid 1000, so apk cannot install anything here.
#
# ## What this migration has NOT proved
#
# musl is not glibc. Thread stack sizes and DNS resolver semantics differ, and
# this project touches both: the Monte Carlo worker pool in
# apps/api/src/workers/pool.ts creates Workers with `resourceLimits`, and the
# Postgres client resolves and connects by hostname. What has been verified is
# that both images build, that trivy reports zero findings against them, that
# `new Worker(…, { resourceLimits: { maxOldGenerationSizeMb } })` runs clean, and
# that ICU is full (`process.config.variables.icu_small === false`, `de-DE`
# formatting and named time zones correct). A full staging run under load is what
# settles the rest, and it has not happened yet. Do not read the zero in the
# table above as "safe"; read it as "no known-vulnerable OS package".
#
# ## No `# syntax=` directive
#
# Adding one would make every build download a Dockerfile frontend image from a
# mutable tag before it does anything else. Nothing here needs a feature outside
# the built-in frontend, so the trade is easy: no directive, no extra mutable
# dependency in the build path.
#
# ## Stage layout, and why it is not "install, build, prune"
#
#   base       bare pinned image. The runtime stage descends from this, so pnpm
#              and corepack shims never reach the shipped image.
#   toolchain  base + corepack. Every build stage descends from this.
#   source     toolchain + the whole build context.
#   prod-deps  a `--prod` install, then the sources pruned away. Runtime
#              dependencies and manifests only.
#   build      a full install (typescript and @types are devDependencies), then
#              `tsc`.
#   runtime    prod-deps' /app, with the compiled dist directories laid over it.
#
# Two alternatives were tried and rejected, both for reasons worth knowing:
#
#   1. **One install, build, then `pnpm install --prod` to prune.** pnpm 11
#      responds to a mode change by wanting to purge and rebuild node_modules
#      wholesale: without a TTY it aborts with
#      ERR_PNPM_ABORTED_REMOVE_MODULES_DIR_NO_TTY, and with `CI=true` set instead
#      it silently re-downloads the entire tree. Two independent installs from the
#      same lockfile are cheaper — BuildKit runs them in parallel — and far easier
#      to reason about.
#
#   2. **Copying each workspace package.json by name before the sources**, the
#      usual trick for keeping the dependency layer cached across source edits.
#      It does not survive here. pnpm-workspace.yaml sets
#      `verifyDepsBeforeRun: error`, so pnpm compares the workspace structure
#      against the last install before running any script — and the moment
#      somebody adds a `packages/*`, the hand-written COPY list is one project
#      short and every build fails with ERR_PNPM_VERIFY_DEPS_BEFORE_RUN
#      ("the workspace structure has changed since last install"). That happened
#      during the writing of this file, which is how it is worded so confidently.
#      The list is a latent build break waiting on somebody else's commit, so
#      there is no list: `COPY . .` and pay ~12 seconds of install on every source
#      change. Correctness first; the cache was worth less than the trap.
#
# ## Why the runtime stage copies prod-deps' whole /app
#
# `nodeLinker: isolated` (pnpm-workspace.yaml) means node_modules is a farm of
# relative symlinks into `node_modules/.pnpm`. Those links only resolve if the
# directory structure is reproduced at the same path, which is why every stage
# uses /app and why the runtime copies the tree in one piece rather than
# cherry-picking package directories. Cherry-picking also fails open: the day
# somebody adds a runtime dependency to `packages/poker-engine`, a hand-written
# list of COPY lines quietly ships an image that cannot resolve it. Copying
# prod-deps' /app wholesale means new dependencies are included automatically.
#
# What that tree contains besides node_modules: the workspace package.json files
# (Node needs them for `"type": "module"` and the `exports` maps), the lockfile,
# pnpm-workspace.yaml and .npmrc. No secrets — .npmrc holds only registry URLs,
# and .dockerignore excludes `.env` and `.env.*` from the build context entirely —
# at every directory depth, not just the context root. That distinction used to
# matter: the root-anchored spelling let `apps/*/.env` through. See that file.
#
# ## What is deliberately NOT in the image
#
# TypeScript sources, tests, EVERY tsconfig (including `tsconfig.test.json`, which
# the prune's glob used to miss), all of apps/web except its package.json, and
# `apps/api/scripts/` — all removed by the prune in the prod-deps stage. Because
# that prune is mostly a deny list rather than an allow list, its worst failure is
# an image slightly larger than necessary, never a broken one. That is the right
# trade, and it is also why it drifted: a fat image is not a red build, so
# `apps/api/tsconfig.test.json`, `apps/web/next.config.ts` and
# `apps/web/next-env.d.ts` sat in the released image without anything complaining.
# The prune's own comments record which line each of those slipped through.
#
# Migrations are therefore run from the host (`pnpm run db:migrate`) rather than
# from this container. If you want them run in-cluster instead, add
# `COPY --from=build /app/apps/api/scripts /app/apps/api/scripts` to the runtime
# stage — and expect a review conversation about giving the serving container
# schema-change rights.
#
# ## Signals
#
# There is no dumb-init or tini in here, and no package was added for one.
# `docker run --init` / `init: true` in docker-compose.yml supplies Docker's own
# PID 1, which reaps zombies and forwards SIGTERM. See the comment in
# docker-compose.yml.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FROM node:24.18.0-alpine3.24@sha256:a0b9bf06e4e6193cf7a0f58816cc935ff8c2a908f81e6f1a95432d679c54fbfd AS base
WORKDIR /app

FROM base AS toolchain
# corepack activates the exact pnpm named in the root package.json's
# `packageManager` field, and verifies it against the sha256 recorded there. That
# is the whole reason this is corepack and not `npm i -g pnpm`.
ENV COREPACK_ENABLE_DOWNLOAD_PROMPT=0 \
    NPM_CONFIG_UPDATE_NOTIFIER=false
RUN corepack enable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# source: the whole build context.
#
# .dockerignore already keeps node_modules, dist, .next, coverage, .git, deploy,
# docs, markdown and — importantly — .env and .env.* out of it, at every depth, so
# this is source plus manifests and nothing sensitive.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM toolchain AS source
COPY . .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# prod-deps: runtime dependencies for the API and its workspace dependencies.
#
# `--filter "@prl/api..."` is "@prl/api and everything it depends on", which
# excludes apps/web and packages/ui — this image has no business containing Next
# or React. `--ignore-scripts` is belt and braces: pnpm-workspace.yaml already
# sets `ignoreScripts: true`, and passing the flag means the build does not
# depend on that file continuing to say so.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM source AS prod-deps
RUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --prod --ignore-scripts --filter "@prl/api..."
# Everything the runtime does not execute. A deny list, so its worst case is a
# slightly fat image rather than a missing module: node_modules and every
# package.json stay, because Node needs them to resolve `"type": "module"` and the
# `exports` maps. `packages/content/data` also stays — the compiled content
# package reads its authored JSON from disk at runtime.
# The markdown lines are belt and braces over .dockerignore, which is where the
# real fix lives. `*.md` there was anchored to the context root, so it removed
# README.md and nothing else, and apps/web/AGENTS.md and apps/web/CLAUDE.md — the
# repository's instructions to coding agents — shipped inside the web image.
# .dockerignore now excludes markdown at every depth; this repeats the claim
# inside the build so that a future change to the ignore file cannot quietly put
# prose back into a layer without also passing this line.
#
# Two things on this list were wrong and were found in the released image
# prl-api@sha256:3854174f…d92e36f, under a .dockerignore that says the tsconfigs
# and the test trees "are removed inside the images by the prune stages":
#
#   * `apps/*/tsconfig.json` does not match `tsconfig.test.json`. Every
#     `tsconfig.test.json` in the repository survived — apps/api's and all five
#     packages'. `tsconfig*.json` is the spelling that means what the line meant.
#   * The app-level entries all name a SUBDIRECTORY (src, test, scripts, public)
#     and nothing at the app root, so apps/web/next.config.ts and
#     apps/web/next-env.d.ts shipped in the API image — a Next config in an image
#     whose own stage header, a few lines up, says it "has no business containing
#     Next or React".
#
# The second one is fixed with an allow list for exactly one directory rather than
# by adding two more names to the deny list, because adding names is what already
# failed: this image executes NOTHING from apps/web, and the only reason the
# directory exists here at all is that Node and pnpm want the manifest to resolve
# the workspace. So keep package.json and delete the rest, and the next root-level
# file somebody adds to the web app cannot repeat this. Everywhere else the deny
# list stands, for the reason in the header — its worst case is a fat image.
#
# `docker-compose*.yml`, not the two names it used to spell out. The deny list
# named `docker-compose.yml` and `docker-compose.staging.yml` and there is a
# third: prl-api@sha256:acd1139c and prl-web@sha256:6a63e44f both shipped
# `/app/docker-compose.observability.yml` — measured with `docker export`, the
# whole Prometheus/Alertmanager/blackbox overlay including the metrics-token mount
# path and the internal service topology. Nothing failed, because
# `release-artifacts.mjs`'s image probe looks for `*.md` and `.env*`. A glob
# cannot drift the way three hand-written names did. `.gitignore` and
# `.dockerignore` go with them for the same reason they are in .dockerignore's
# "prose" section: they are a map of where this deployment keeps its secrets, and
# nothing in a runtime image reads either one.
RUN rm -rf \
      apps/*/src apps/*/test apps/*/scripts apps/*/public \
      packages/*/src packages/*/test \
      apps/*/tsconfig*.json packages/*/tsconfig*.json tsconfig.base.json \
      biome.json db .github scripts Dockerfile.api Dockerfile.web \
      docker-compose*.yml .dockerignore .gitignore deploy \
      ./*.md apps/*/*.md packages/*/*.md \
 && find apps/web -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 ! -name package.json -exec rm -rf {} +

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# build: full install, then compile.
#
# NODE_ENV is deliberately NOT production in this stage. pnpm treats
# NODE_ENV=production as an implicit `--prod`, which would omit typescript and
# @types/node and break the build with a confusing "cannot find name 'process'".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM source AS build
RUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts --filter "@prl/api..."
# One recursive, topologically-ordered build: each workspace dependency emits its
# dist/*.d.ts before the package that consumes it is compiled. Filtering to
# "@prl/api..." rather than running `build:packages` matters — the latter includes
# packages/ui, whose typecheck needs the React types that this filtered install
# deliberately left out.
RUN pnpm --filter "@prl/api..." run build
# Reduce this stage to exactly what the runtime stage should lay over prod-deps:
# every package's `dist/` (and packages/content/data), and apps/api/dist. The
# node_modules go first and they MUST go — this stage's tree is a FULL install, and
# copying it over prod-deps' would silently reinstate typescript and every @types
# package in the shipped image, undoing the entire point of having two installs.
#
# Deleting here rather than listing what to keep in the runtime stage is the whole
# fix for a defect this file used to have: the runtime stage named each package's
# dist in a hand-written COPY line, and the day apps/api gained a dependency on
# @prl/poker-ai the list was one line short. The image built, passed every layer
# check, and died at boot with ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND on
# /app/apps/api/node_modules/@prl/poker-ai/dist/index.js — the "fails open" failure
# this file's own header warns about, in this file. A deny list here and one
# directory-wide COPY below means a new workspace package is included automatically.
#
# `tsconfig*.json`, not `tsconfig.json`, for the same reason as in the prod-deps
# stage: /app/packages is copied from HERE into the runtime, so the narrow glob
# left a `tsconfig.test.json` in every workspace package of the shipped image.
RUN rm -rf \
      packages/*/node_modules packages/*/src packages/*/test packages/*/tsconfig*.json \
      apps/api/node_modules apps/api/src apps/api/test apps/api/scripts apps/api/tsconfig*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# runtime
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM base AS runtime

# NODE_ENV=production only here, where nothing installs. PORT is the contract
# with docker-compose.yml and the healthcheck below.
#
# HOST=0.0.0.0 is not a convenience, it is the difference between a container that
# serves traffic and one that does not. `config.ts` defaults HOST to 127.0.0.1 — the
# right default for a dev server on a laptop, and wrong for every container, because a
# process listening on loopback inside its own network namespace is unreachable from
# the compose network and from a published port. Setting it here rather than only in
# docker-compose.yml means `docker run` of this image is correct too, and it mirrors
# HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0 in Dockerfile.web. 0.0.0.0 is safe precisely because the network
# namespace, not the bind address, is the isolation boundary; the port is only
# reachable where something publishes or routes it.
#
# An operator who genuinely wants loopback-only (a sidecar sharing a namespace) sets
# HOST back to 127.0.0.1 and the healthcheck below will still be honest about it.
ENV NODE_ENV=production \
    PORT=3001 \
    HOST=0.0.0.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Remove npm, corepack and yarn from the RUNTIME image.
#
# The official Node image ships npm at /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm with its
# own bundled dependency tree, and corepack beside it. This project uses neither
# at runtime: the API's command is `node apps/api/dist/server.js`, the web app's
# is a pnpm-generated bin shim that execs node, and the header of this file
# explains at length why running pnpm at container start would fail anyway.
#
# Two reasons to delete them, one measured and one structural.
#
# **Measured.** `trivy image` against a bookworm-slim-era build reported eight
# HIGH and CRITICAL vulnerabilities whose package path was
# `usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/...` — brace-expansion, ip-address,
# tar and undici, all bundled inside npm, all with fixes available upstream and
# none of them reachable from anything this image runs. They were, at the time,
# the ONLY findings in the image with an available fix; everything else was a
# Debian package Debian had marked `fix_deferred` or `will_not_fix`. Deleting a
# package manager nothing uses removed every actionable finding. That measurement
# is history now — the base is Alpine and the whole image scans at zero — but the
# lines stay, because "the scanner currently finds nothing" is not a reason to
# ship a bundled dependency tree nothing executes.
#
# **Structural.** A package manager in a production container is a downloader and
# an arbitrary-code executor sitting next to the application, and this image runs
# with a read-only root filesystem precisely because we do not want either.
#
# **The three yarn paths are new, and they are here because the base changed.**
# bookworm-slim had no yarn at all; `node:24.18.0-alpine3.24` ships yarn 1.22.22
# unpacked at /opt/yarn-v1.22.22 with /usr/local/bin/yarn and
# /usr/local/bin/yarnpkg symlinked to it. Carrying it over unnoticed would have
# quietly reinstated exactly the thing the paragraph above deletes: a second
# package manager, in an image whose stated posture is that it has none. Whoever
# next changes the base should re-run `ls /usr/local/bin /opt` on it rather than
# trusting this list — it is a deny list, and the base decides what it misses.
#
# `npm`, `npx`, `yarn` and `yarnpkg` under /usr/local/bin are symlinks into the
# directories above, so they are removed by name rather than left dangling. Run
# before `USER node` because /usr/local and /opt are root-owned; nothing after
# this line needs any of these tools.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN rm -rf /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm /usr/local/lib/node_modules/corepack \
      /usr/local/bin/npm /usr/local/bin/npx /usr/local/bin/corepack \
      /opt/yarn-v1.22.22 /usr/local/bin/yarn /usr/local/bin/yarnpkg

COPY --from=prod-deps --chown=node:node /app /app
# Two directory-wide copies, not a per-package list. COPY merges into an existing
# directory, so prod-deps' node_modules symlink farm survives underneath and the
# compiled output lands on top of it.
#
# packages/ carries every workspace package's dist/ — whichever packages those are
# today — plus packages/content/data, which stays because content keeps its authored
# JSON outside src/ (tsc will not emit a file from above rootDir) and the compiled
# dist reads it from disk at runtime. Without it every content route 500s on a
# missing glossary.
COPY --from=build --chown=node:node /app/packages /app/packages
COPY --from=build --chown=node:node /app/apps/api/dist /app/apps/api/dist

# uid/gid 1000, present in the official Node images. Never root: a process that
# cannot write to its own code cannot be persuaded to modify it.
USER node

EXPOSE 3001

# `node -e` rather than curl or wget. On bookworm-slim the reason was that
# neither existed and adding one was a new package in the runtime image for no
# reason; on Alpine busybox does provide wget, and the probe stays `node -e`
# anyway, because the better half of that argument never depended on
# availability. node is the one binary this image is guaranteed to have — it is
# a Node image — so the probe cannot be broken by a base change, and the exec
# form means there is no shell in the probe either. A wget probe would also need
# `CMD-SHELL` to interpolate the hostname, putting a shell back into the health
# path of a container that otherwise has no reason to start one.
#
# The probe targets `os.hostname()`, NOT 127.0.0.1. Docker resolves a container's own
# hostname to its address on the container network, so this asks the question that
# matters — "is the server reachable at the address other containers and the published
# port use?" — instead of the weaker "is something listening on loopback?". A probe on
# 127.0.0.1 reports healthy for a server bound to 127.0.0.1, which is exactly the
# failure mode that shipped: healthy, and unreachable from anywhere. This probe fails
# for that configuration, so a missing HOST cannot masquerade as a working service.
#
# start-period covers migration waits and connection-pool warm-up; the interval
# is deliberately unhurried, because a healthcheck that polls hard is a load
# generator pointed at your own service.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --start-period=30s --retries=3 \
  CMD ["node", "-e", "fetch(`http://${require('node:os').hostname()}:${process.env.PORT ?? 3001}/api/v1/health`).then((r)=>process.exit(r.ok?0:1)).catch(()=>process.exit(1))"]

CMD ["node", "apps/api/dist/server.js"]
